INT. LIGHTHOUSE LAMP ROOM - STORM - NIGHT

A circular room of glass and brass. The great lens turns slowly, throwing beams across black water. Rain slaps the windows. Five people crowd the narrow gallery, wet coats steaming.

INSPECTOR HOLLOWAY BRAITHWAITE, 50s, trench coat, notebook, studies the room. KEEPER MARIGOLD FINCH, 60s, grips a lantern. DR. ANSELM OYEDELE, 40s, holds a medical bag. SISTER CORDELIA, 30s, fingers a rosary. And the boat captain, DESDEMONA QUILL, 50s, arms folded by the stairs.

A body lies under a tarp at the center of the floor.

BRAITHWAITE
(to the room)
Nobody leaves until I say.

QUILL
The tide says otherwise.

BRAITHWAITE
Then the tide can wait.

FINCH
(muttering)
Fourteen years and not one drowned. Now this.

OYEDELE
(to Braithwaite)
He wasn't drowned.

BRAITHWAITE
How do you know?

OYEDELE
Because there's no water in his lungs.

A beat. The lens beam sweeps across the faces, one by one.

CORDELIA
(to herself)
I saw nothing.

A radio on the wall crackles.

COASTGUARD (V.O.)
(over radio, thin)
Lighthouse, this is Coastguard. Storm has closed the channel. Repeat, no boats until dawn.

BRAITHWAITE
(to the radio)
Understood. Hold position.

COASTGUARD (V.O.)
(over radio)
Is anyone injured?

BRAITHWAITE
(glancing at the tarp)
Not anymore.

He switches off the radio. The beam sweeps again. This time he is watching Finch.

BRAITHWAITE (CONT'D)
(to the whole room)
I'll ask once. Who was at the lamp when it went dark?

Nobody answers. Then, far above, the lens stops turning.
